Needs a build made with `THIESSEN_EXPERIMENTAL=1 R CMD INSTALL r`; the
[experimental](experimental.html) page has the catalogue and the rule.

Draft: the skeleton of the page. `tools/articles.sh` skips it until the
`draft` line leaves the front matter. Shorter than the feature
articles: no seeds table, no comparator, and no boundary figure beyond
the reproduction check, since the conformance tests are these
components' evidence and the page is the picture the reference cannot
give.

## Introduction

The assignment rule is nearest centre over the active columns under the
declared metric; the metric changes which rows share a cell and nothing
else in the sampler. Six experimental metrics and one inclusion prior
are reached through `geometry_params(metric = list(...))` with
`"manhattan"`, `list(minkowski = list(p = 3))`, `"cosine"`,
`list(gower = list(kind = "numeric"))`, `"mahalanobis"` with
`precision =`, and a `group` label for a composite; and through
`structure_params(inclusion = weighted_inclusion(w))`. Statement:
`docs/models.md`, "Distances" and the experimental entries.

## Example

```{r example}
source("_setup.R")
data <- smooth_surface(n = 300, sd = 0.1, seed = 1)
grid <- as.matrix(expand.grid(x1 = seq(0, 1, length.out = 80),
                              x2 = seq(0, 1, length.out = 80)))
one <- function(metric) thiessen_control(
  tessellations = 1, general_params = schedule,
  mean_params = term_params(geometry = geometry_params(metric = metric))
)
metrics <- list(
  euclidean = NULL,
  manhattan = list("manhattan", "manhattan"),
  minkowski = list(list(minkowski = list(p = 3)), list(minkowski = list(p = 3))),
  cosine = list("cosine", "cosine")
)
cells <- lapply(metrics, function(metric) {
  fit <- thiessen(data$train$x, data$train$y, one(metric), seed = 1, chains = 1)
  predict(fit, grid, type = "draws")[300, ]
})
```

A one-tessellation fit and a single kept draw of `predict(type =
"draws")` over a grid is piecewise constant over that draw's cells, so
plotting it shows the cells. Same seed and data under every metric.

## What it shows

Figure 1, cells: one panel per metric on the same design, Euclidean,
Manhattan, Minkowski p = 3, cosine, Mahalanobis with the sample
precision on a correlated design, a composite with Manhattan on x1 and
Euclidean on x2, and Gower on a mixed numeric and categorical design as
a seventh panel with the categorical column as colour.

```{r cells}
```

Figure 2, weighted inclusion: `variable_inclusion()` on Friedman #1
with p = 10 under uniform weights, under weights favouring the five true
columns, and with one column at weight zero.

```{r weights}
```

Reproduction: the difference of the draws between Euclidean and
Minkowski p = 2, and between Euclidean and an all-Euclidean composite,
both exactly zero, as the check that the extensions leave the published
chain alone.

```{r reproduction}
```

One paragraph per metric on when it applies and its caveat: cosine has
no triangle inequality and wants direction data; Gower does not weight
missing values; Mahalanobis uses the principal submatrix, not the
conditional precision; the composite's axioms are those of its weakest
member.

## References

Stone, A. and Gosling, J. P. (2025). AddiVortes: (Bayesian) additive
Voronoi tessellations. *Journal of Computational and Graphical
Statistics* 34(3), 859-871. doi:10.1080/10618600.2024.2414104
